Like...\nTurn 54. TUTOR: I guess you have to show which, I don't know, like what two numbers to make that total number. So which diagram...\nTurn 55. STUDENT: So it's D?\nTurn 56. TUTOR: Um, well, remember the big box should be the total number, and the total number is sixty-seven.\nTurn 57. STUDENT: Sixty-seven. The answer's forty-five.\nTurn 58. TUTOR: Well, yeah, yeah, but like the total number in the beginning is sixty-seven, so that should go in the big box.\nTurn 59. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 7 seconds]\nTurn 59. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Sarah has 68 paperclips. She gives 23 of them away to her friend. Which diagram would be used to find how many she has left?' (start)] Problem is shown with four bar model diagrams as options A, B, C, and D.\nTurn 59. STUDENT: The answer's forty-five.\nTurn 60. TUTOR: Well, yeah, yeah, but like the total number in the beginning is sixty-seven, so that should go in the big box.\nTurn 61. STUDENT: Oh, so it's, um, B.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
